      ":question/worked-solution": "A coefficient matrix, obtained by omitting the final column of its augmented matrix, is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\nI. All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\nII. Each leading entry of a nonzero row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the preceding nonzero row.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The coefficient matrix of $A$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry in row $2$, column $1$ is in the same column as the leading entry in row $1$, column $1$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}10 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n- The coefficient matrix of $B$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry in row $3$, column $2$ is in the same column as the leading entry in row $2$, column $2$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 5 & -9 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 11 & 5 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -7 & 6\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n- The coefficient matrix of $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}8 & 11 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 9\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, select $A$, $B$.",
